To configure comments in a web application using ASP.NET Core, you can follow these steps:

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Builder;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;

public class Startup
{
    public Startup(IConfiguration configuration)
    {
        Configuration = configuration;
    }

    public IConfiguration Configuration { get; }

    // This method gets called by the runtime. Use this method to add services to the container.
    public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
    {
        services.AddTransient<CommentsService>();
    }

    // This method gets called by the runtime. Use this method to configure the HTTP request pipeline.
    public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
    {
        app.UseRouting();
        app.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
        {
            endpoints.MapControllers();
        });
    }
}

public class CommentsService
{
    private readonly List<Comment> comments = new List<Comment>();

    public Comment CreateComment(Comment comment)
    {
        comments.Add(comment);
        return comment;
    }

    public List<Comment> GetComments()
    {
        return comments;
    }
}

public class Comment
{
    public string Text { get; set; }
    public long Timestamp { get; set; }
}
```

In this example, we have a `CommentsService` that is responsible for creating and retrieving comments. We use the `AddTransient` method to add an instance of `CommentsService` to the DI container.

We also define a `Comment` class to represent individual comments.

The `Configure` method is used to configure the HTTP request pipeline, including mapping controllers and routing requests.

```csharp
[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class CommentsController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly CommentsService _commentsService;

    public CommentsController(CommentsService commentsService)
    {
        _commentsService = commentsService;
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult CreateComment([FromBody] Comment comment)
    {
        return Ok(_commentsService.CreateComment(comment));
    }

    [HttpGet]
    public IActionResult GetComments()
    {
        var comments = _commentsService.GetComments();
        return Ok(comments);
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a `CommentsController` that handles HTTP requests related to comments. The `CreateComment` method creates a new comment and returns it in the response body, while the `GetComments` method retrieves all existing comments and returns them in the response body.

Please note that this is just one possible way to configure comments in an ASP.NET Core web application. Depending on your specific requirements, you may need to modify or extend this example.
