To configure comments in a Django project, you can follow these steps:

```python
# settings.py
INSTALLED_APPS = [
    # ...
    'comments.apps.CommentsConfig',
]

# models.py
from django.db import models

class Comment(models.Model):
    text = models.CharField(max_length=255)
    timestamp = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)

    def __str__(self):
        return self.text
```

In this example, we have a `Comment` model that represents individual comments. We define two fields: `text` and `timestamp`. The `timestamp` field is an automatic field that gets updated when the comment is created.

```python
# views.py
from django.shortcuts import render, redirect
from .models import Comment

def create_comment(request):
    if request.method == 'POST':
        text = request.POST.get('text')
        timestamp = datetime.now()
        comment = Comment(text=text, timestamp=timestamp)
        comment.save()
        return redirect('comments_list')
    else:
        return render(request, 'create_comment.html')

def comments_list(request):
    comments = Comment.objects.all()
    return render(request, 'comments_list.html', {'comments': comments})
```

In this example, we have two views: `create_comment` and `comments_list`. The `create_comment` view handles the creation of new comments, while the `comments_list` view displays a list of all existing comments.

```html
<!-- create_comment.html -->
<form method="post">
    {% csrf_token %}
    <textarea name="text"></textarea>
    <button type="submit">Create Comment</button>
</form>

<!-- comments_list.html -->
<h1>Comments List</h1>
<ul>
    {% for comment in comments %}
        <li>{{ comment.text }} ({{ comment.timestamp }})
            <a href="{% url 'edit_comment' comment.id %}">Edit</a>
            <a href="{% url 'delete_comment' comment.id %}">Delete</a>
        </li>
    {% endfor %}
</ul>

<!-- edit_comment.html -->
<form method="post">
    {% csrf_token %}
    <textarea name="text">{{ comment.text }}</textarea>
    <button type="submit">Update Comment</button>
</form>

<!-- delete_comment.html -->
<form method="post">
    {% csrf_token %}
    <button type="submit">Delete Comment</button>
</form>
```

In this example, we have four templates: `create_comment`, `comments_list`, `edit_comment`, and `delete_comment`. The `create_comment` template allows users to create new comments, while the `comments_list` template displays a list of all existing comments. The `edit_comment` and `delete_comment` templates allow users to edit or delete individual comments.

Please note that this is just one possible way to configure comments in a Django project. Depending on your specific requirements, you may need to modify or extend this example.
